WEBVTT

00:00.540 --> 00:04.650
We're asked to simplify
log base 3 of 27x.

00:04.650 --> 00:06.400
And frankly, this is
already quite simple.

00:06.400 --> 00:09.620
But I'm assuming they want us
to use some logarithm properties

00:09.620 --> 00:11.830
and manipulate this in
some way, maybe to actually

00:11.830 --> 00:13.550
make it a little bit
more complicated.

00:13.550 --> 00:15.260
But let's give our
best shot at it.

00:15.260 --> 00:17.740
So the logarithm property
that jumps out at me--

00:17.740 --> 00:20.440
because this right over here--
we're saying what power do we

00:20.440 --> 00:23.050
have to raise 3 to to get 27x?

00:23.050 --> 00:26.350
27x is the same
thing as 27 times x.

00:26.350 --> 00:30.210
And so the logarithm property it
seems like they want us to use

00:30.210 --> 00:36.670
is log base-- let me write
it-- log base b of a times

00:36.670 --> 00:40.280
c-- I'll write it this way--
log base b of a times c.

00:40.280 --> 00:44.850
This is equal to the
logarithm base b of a plus

00:44.850 --> 00:48.430
the logarithm base b of c.

00:48.430 --> 00:51.090
And this comes straight out
of the exponent properties

00:51.090 --> 00:54.757
that if you have two exponents,
two with the same base,

00:54.757 --> 00:55.840
you can add the exponents.

00:55.840 --> 00:57.620
So let me make that a
little bit clearer to you.

00:57.620 --> 00:58.850
And if this part is
a little confusing,

00:58.850 --> 01:00.120
the important part
for this example

01:00.120 --> 01:01.750
is that you know
how to apply this.

01:01.750 --> 01:04.269
But it's even better if
you know the intuition.

01:04.269 --> 01:10.430
So let's say that log base b
of a times c is equal to x.

01:10.430 --> 01:13.680
So this thing right over
here evaluates to x.

01:13.680 --> 01:17.340
Let's say that this thing
right over here evaluates to y.

01:17.340 --> 01:22.070
So log base b of
a is equal to y.

01:22.070 --> 01:26.580
And let's say that this thing
over here evaluates to z.

01:26.580 --> 01:32.820
So log base b of
c is equal to z.

01:32.820 --> 01:37.860
Now, what we know is, this thing
right over here or this thing

01:37.860 --> 01:43.290
right over here tells
us that b to the x power

01:43.290 --> 01:46.950
is equal to a times c.

01:46.950 --> 01:49.550
Now, this right over
here is telling us

01:49.550 --> 01:51.743
that b to the y
power is equal to a.

01:54.620 --> 01:58.240
And this over here is telling
us that b to the z power

01:58.240 --> 01:59.670
is equal to c.

01:59.670 --> 02:01.610
Let me do that in
that same green.

02:01.610 --> 02:03.840
So I'm just writing
the same truth.

02:03.840 --> 02:05.810
I'm writing it as an
exponential function

02:05.810 --> 02:09.210
or exponential equation, instead
of a logarithmic equation.

02:09.210 --> 02:12.800
So b to the zth
power is equal to c.

02:12.800 --> 02:18.370
This is the same statement
or the same truth

02:18.370 --> 02:19.860
said in a different way.

02:19.860 --> 02:23.190
And this is the same truth
said in a different way.

02:23.190 --> 02:26.590
Well, if we know that
a is equal to this,

02:26.590 --> 02:34.030
is equal to b to the y,
and c is equal to bz,

02:34.030 --> 02:37.400
then we can write
b to the x power

02:37.400 --> 02:42.540
is equal to b to the y
power-- that's what a is.

02:42.540 --> 02:46.275
We know that already--
times b to the z power.

02:49.390 --> 02:53.830
And we know from our
exponent properties

02:53.830 --> 02:56.650
that if we take b to
the y times b to the z,

02:56.650 --> 03:00.340
this is the same thing
as b to the-- I'll

03:00.340 --> 03:04.260
do it in a neutral color--
b to the y plus z power.

03:04.260 --> 03:06.770
This comes straight out of
our exponent properties.

03:06.770 --> 03:08.764
And so if b to
the y plus z power

03:08.764 --> 03:10.930
is the same thing as b to
the x power, that tells us

03:10.930 --> 03:14.650
that x must be
equal to y plus z.

03:18.720 --> 03:21.370
If this is confusing to you,
don't worry about it too much.

03:21.370 --> 03:23.490
The important thing, or at
least the first important thing,

03:23.490 --> 03:24.920
is that you know
how to apply it.

03:24.920 --> 03:26.560
And then you can think about
this a little bit more,

03:26.560 --> 03:28.476
and you can even try it
out with some numbers.

03:28.476 --> 03:30.610
You just have to realize
that logarithms are really

03:30.610 --> 03:31.510
just exponents.

03:31.510 --> 03:33.560
I know when people first would
tell me that, I was like, well,

03:33.560 --> 03:34.393
what does that mean?

03:34.393 --> 03:36.782
But when you
evaluate a logarithm,

03:36.782 --> 03:38.240
you're getting an
exponent that you

03:38.240 --> 03:41.370
would have to raise b
to to get to a times c.

03:41.370 --> 03:44.350
But let's just apply this
property right over here.

03:44.350 --> 03:45.870
So if we apply it
to this one, we

03:45.870 --> 03:50.670
know that log base
3 of 27 times x--

03:50.670 --> 03:56.460
I'll write it that way-- is
equal to log base 3 of 27

03:56.460 --> 04:02.660
plus log base 3 of x.

04:02.660 --> 04:05.510
And then this right over
here, we can evaluate.

04:05.510 --> 04:07.690
This tells us, what
power do I have

04:07.690 --> 04:10.420
to raise 3 to to get to 27?

04:10.420 --> 04:11.710
You could view it as this way.

04:11.710 --> 04:13.060
3 to the ?

04:13.060 --> 04:15.500
is equal to 27.

04:15.500 --> 04:18.350
Well, 3 to the third
power is equal to 27.

04:18.350 --> 04:20.870
3 times 3 is 9 times 3 is 27.

04:20.870 --> 04:23.367
So this right over
here evaluates to 3.

04:23.367 --> 04:24.950
So if we were to
simplify-- or I guess

04:24.950 --> 04:26.320
I wouldn't even call
it simplifying it.

04:26.320 --> 04:28.861
I would just call it expanding
it out or using this property,

04:28.861 --> 04:31.150
because we now have two
terms where we started off

04:31.150 --> 04:31.734
with one term.

04:31.734 --> 04:33.150
Actually, if we
started with this,

04:33.150 --> 04:35.530
I'd say that this is the
more simple version of it.

04:35.530 --> 04:38.590
But when we rewrite it,
this first term becomes 3.

04:41.560 --> 04:46.060
And then we're left with
plus log base 3 of x.

04:46.060 --> 04:48.480
So this is just an
alternate way of writing

04:48.480 --> 04:54.220
this original statement,
log base 3 of 27x.

04:54.220 --> 04:55.830
So once again, not
clear that this

04:55.830 --> 04:58.240
is simpler than this
right over here.

04:58.240 --> 05:02.798
It's just another way of writing
it using logarithm properties.
